## TactiQ OS audit rules.
##
## The events IACS UR E27 item 13 names, as they exist on this image:
##   access control      logins and authentication are recorded by PAM
##                       through libaudit, SELinux denials (AVC) by the
##                       kernel; neither needs a rule here
##   operating system    kernel modules, clock changes
##   backup and restore  installation of an update bundle (rauc)
##   configuration       the writable configuration on /data, in
##                       tactiq-data.rules (loaded later, see there); the
##                       rootfs, /etc included, is dm-verity and cannot
##                       change
##   loss of communication
##                       not applicable: the image has no network link
##                       whose loss could be recorded
##
## Each rule carries a key, so a record can be found with ausearch -k.
##
## Watches are written as syscall rules (-a always,exit -F path= or dir=
## -F perm=), not as -w. For a -w rule auditctl prints "Old style watch
## rules are slower" and, when run as a service, also tries to send that
## line to /dev/log, which auditctl_t may not write: on the bench board
## (2026-11-04) the -w form left one SELinux denial per boot. The syscall
## form loads silently and records the same events.

## Kernel modules
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S init_module,finit_module,delete_module -k modules

## Clock
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S adjtimex,settimeofday,clock_settime -k time

## Update bundles (backup and restore of a slot)
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -F path=/usr/bin/rauc -F perm=x -k update
